In **SPMD (Single Program, Multiple Data)**, **one program runs on all processors**, and each processor works on **its own part of the data**. In practice **most parallel programs are SPMD**.

```c
local_size  = size / p;
local_lower = me * local_size;
local_upper = (me + 1) * local_size - 1;
local_sum = 0;
for (i = local_lower; i <= local_upper; i++)
    local_sum += x[i] * y[i];
Reduce(&local_sum, &global_sum, 0, SUM);
```
- `me` = this processor's id (0 … p−1), `p` = the number of processors.
- Each processor computes the dot product of **its own block** of x and y.
- **Reduce** adds all `local_sum` values together into `global_sum` on processor **0** (a single accumulation).

*Example:* size = 1000, p = 4. Processor me = 2 handles i = 500 … 749.
(The code assumes `size` is divisible by `p`. Otherwise the last processor must also take the remaining elements.)

**Fork-Join:** an existing thread **T1 creates (forks)** a second thread T2, or a group of threads, which run in parallel with it. Later T1 **waits (joins)** until they have finished, and then continues.

- **Arbitrary nesting:** a forked thread can itself fork new threads (e.g. recursive divide-and-conquer).
- The details differ between programming languages and environments.

```c
pthread_t t[4];
for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)                       // fork
    pthread_create(&t[i], NULL, work, &arg[i]);
/* main thread can do its own work here */
for (i = 0; i < 4; i++)                       // join
    pthread_join(t[i], NULL);
/* all four results are now available */
```

*Nested example:* parallel quicksort. After partitioning, fork a thread for the left half, sort the right half yourself, then join.

A is a dense **n × m** matrix, b has length m, and c has length n:   **cᵢ = Σⱼ aᵢⱼ·bⱼ**, i = 1 … n

**1. Scalar products (row-oriented), i outer:** cᵢ is the **dot product of row i with b**.
```c
for (i = 0; i < n; i++) c[i] = 0;
for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < m; j++)
        c[i] = c[i] + A[i][j] * b[j];
```

**2. Linear combination of columns (column-oriented), j outer:** c = Σⱼ bⱼ·(column j of A). Each step adds column j, scaled by bⱼ.
```c
for (j = 0; j < m; j++)
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
        c[i] = c[i] + A[i][j] * b[j];
```

**Both give the same result.** The loops can be exchanged because of **data independence** (only the order of the additions into c[i] changes).

*Side note on locality:* C stores A row by row. Version 1 walks along rows (good spatial locality), while version 2 jumps m elements per access.

SPMD program for processor Pₖ (k = 1 … p):
```c
local_n = n / p;
for (i = 0; i < local_n; i++) local_c[i] = 0;
for (i = 0; i < local_n; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < m; j++)
        local_c[i] = local_c[i] + local_A[i][j] * b[j];
MPI_Allgather(local_c, local_n, MPI_DOUBLE,
              global_c, local_n, MPI_DOUBLE, comm);
```
- **local_A** (local_n × m) holds this processor's rows: `local_A[i][j] = A[i + (k−1)·n/p][j]`
- **b** is replicated, so every processor has all m elements.
- **local_c** (length local_n): the processor's part of the result, computed with ordinary scalar products using **local indices** 0 … local_n−1.
- **MPI_Allgather = multi-broadcast:** every processor sends its local_n values, and every processor receives everything, in order of rank. Afterwards `global_c[i + (k−1)·n/p] = local_c[i]` on **all** processors.

*Example:* n = 8, p = 4, so local_n = 2. P3 (k = 3) owns rows 4 and 5 (0-based) and computes c[4] and c[5].

**SPMD program with a distribution of the computations**, with **no explicit distribution of the data**: A, b and c all stay in shared memory. Each process has a **private variable k** (its id).

```c
local_n = n / p;
for (i = 0; i < local_n; i++) c[i + (k-1)*local_n] = 0;
for (i = 0; i < local_n; i++)
    for (j = 0; j < m; j++)
        c[i + (k-1)*local_n] =
            c[i + (k-1)*local_n] + A[i + (k-1)*local_n][j] * b[j];
synch();
```
- **Each process computes n/p components of c**, using **the corresponding n/p rows of A**.
- **No access conflicts:** every process **writes only its own part of c**, and A and b are **only read**. No two processes write the same location, so **no locks are needed**.
- **`synch()` is a barrier.** No process may use c until **all** processes have finished their parts.

**Compared with distributed memory:** there is no MPI_Allgather, because c is already visible to everyone. The barrier takes the place of the communication.
